WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.185
Lesson 2.3.

00:00:02.185 --> 00:00:03.750
Providing liquidity.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.940
By the end, you'll estimate an LP position's fee

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:10.325
income from real pool data, before you ever deposit.

00:00:11.625 --> 00:00:12.915
Here's the plan.

00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:15.542
What providing liquidity actually does to your

00:00:15.542 --> 00:00:17.910
position, not just the fees it earns.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.527
The fee A.P.R. formula, spelled out piece by piece,

00:00:21.527 --> 00:00:25.096
and why thirty-day volume is the number to use.

00:00:25.096 --> 00:00:28.094
And a full worked example, with real numbers,

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:32.070
showing exactly how a ten-thousand-dollar deposit turns into

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.286
an actual daily and annual return.

00:00:35.586 --> 00:00:37.111
What LPing actually does.

00:00:38.411 --> 00:00:41.566
Here's the mechanics of becoming a liquidity provider.

00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:43.649
You deposit both tokens, in the

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:45.706
pool's ratio, not just one.

00:00:45.706 --> 00:00:48.529
In return, you receive a share, a token,

00:00:48.529 --> 00:00:51.695
or an N.F.T. if it's a concentrated position,

00:00:51.695 --> 00:00:54.336
covered in Lesson two point seven.

00:00:54.336 --> 00:00:57.040
That share entitles you to your proportional cut

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.137
of every swap fee the pool earns, for

00:00:59.137 --> 00:01:00.406
as long as you hold it.

00:01:01.706 --> 00:01:03.322
Here's the part that's easy to

00:01:03.322 --> 00:01:05.081
skip past, and shouldn't be.

00:01:05.081 --> 00:01:07.128
When you provide liquidity, you're not

00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:09.370
a passive lender collecting interest.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.705
You become the counterparty to every single

00:01:11.705 --> 00:01:13.810
trade that goes through that pool.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.651
When a trader buys the token that's rising, they're

00:01:16.651 --> 00:01:19.245
buying it from the pool, meaning from you.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.492
That's exactly where impermanent loss, covered fully in

00:01:22.492 --> 00:01:24.985
Lesson two point four, actually comes from.

00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:28.751
Worth naming the two position types you'll

00:01:28.751 --> 00:01:31.561
actually choose between, even though this lesson

00:01:31.561 --> 00:01:33.410
focuses on the simpler one.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:37.178
A full-range position covers every possible price, simple to

00:01:37.178 --> 00:01:40.320
reason about, earning fees on the whole curve.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.725
A concentrated position, an NFT instead of a token,

00:01:43.725 --> 00:01:46.803
covers only a price band you choose, which can

00:01:46.803 --> 00:01:49.881
earn far more fee income inside that band, but

00:01:49.881 --> 00:01:53.090
earns nothing at all once price moves outside it.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.059
Here's what actually happens when you eventually withdraw, since

00:01:58.059 --> 00:02:01.295
it's the moment your changed token mix becomes real.

00:02:01.295 --> 00:02:04.069
You redeem your share, your L.P. token

00:02:04.069 --> 00:02:06.360
or N.F.T., back to the pool.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.994
The pool returns whatever mix of the two

00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:12.142
tokens your share is currently worth, at today's

00:02:12.142 --> 00:02:15.590
ratio, not the ratio you originally deposited at.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.195
And you collect the accumulated fees your share

00:02:18.195 --> 00:02:19.878
has earned, on top of that mix.

00:02:21.178 --> 00:02:24.031
One practical option worth knowing before you

00:02:24.031 --> 00:02:26.873
assume you always need both tokens ready.

00:02:26.873 --> 00:02:30.114
Some protocols accept a single-sided deposit, and

00:02:30.114 --> 00:02:32.448
swap half of it internally to build

00:02:32.448 --> 00:02:34.498
your pool position for you.

00:02:34.498 --> 00:02:37.534
The trade-off: that internal swap has its own

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:40.372
price impact and fee, paid once, up front,

00:02:40.372 --> 00:02:44.001
versus depositing both tokens yourself, which needs no

00:02:44.001 --> 00:02:46.641
internal swap at all, but does need you

00:02:46.641 --> 00:02:48.753
to already hold the right ratio.

00:02:50.053 --> 00:02:51.143
Quick check.

00:02:51.143 --> 00:02:54.850
When you provide liquidity, what does becoming “the counterparty

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.588
to every trade” actually mean for your position?

00:03:02.008 --> 00:03:04.650
The answer: as traders move the price, your

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.293
token mix changes with it, since you're the

00:03:07.293 --> 00:03:10.596
one supplying whichever token they're buying, which is

00:03:10.596 --> 00:03:13.118
exactly where impermanent loss comes from.

00:03:14.418 --> 00:03:15.773
The fee APR formula.

00:03:17.073 --> 00:03:19.067
Here's the formula, broken into the

00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:21.362
four numbers that actually build it.

00:03:21.362 --> 00:03:23.797
Daily trading volume through the pool.

00:03:23.797 --> 00:03:25.996
Multiplied by the pool's fee tier,

00:03:25.996 --> 00:03:27.867
the cut every trade pays.

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:30.490
Multiplied by your share of the pool, your

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.352
deposit divided by the pool's total value.

00:03:33.352 --> 00:03:36.962
And annualised, multiplied by three hundred sixty-five,

00:03:36.962 --> 00:03:39.025
to turn a daily number into the

00:03:39.025 --> 00:03:41.539
yearly rate everyone actually compares.

00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:46.405
One simplification worth knowing, since it makes the mental

00:03:46.405 --> 00:03:49.974
math far easier for a full-range position specifically.

00:03:49.974 --> 00:03:52.753
Your share of a full-range pool is just your

00:03:52.753 --> 00:03:56.079
deposit divided by the pool's total value locked.

00:03:56.079 --> 00:03:59.996
Once you substitute that in, the whole formula collapses

00:03:59.996 --> 00:04:03.363
to one line: daily volume, times fee tier, times

00:04:03.363 --> 00:04:07.692
three hundred sixty-five, divided by T.V.L. Nothing about your

00:04:07.692 --> 00:04:10.784
specific deposit size needs to appear at all.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:14.842
And here's exactly why this lesson insists on

00:04:14.842 --> 00:04:18.380
thirty-day average volume, not today's number, since using

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.199
the wrong one quietly wrecks the estimate.

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:24.394
A single volatile day can spike volume far above

00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:27.914
normal, making the fee APR look unusually good, right

00:04:27.914 --> 00:04:31.539
when impermanent loss is also running at its worst.

00:04:31.539 --> 00:04:34.188
A thirty-day average smooths that out, giving

00:04:34.188 --> 00:04:36.491
you a number that actually represents a

00:04:36.491 --> 00:04:38.219
typical day, not the best one.

00:04:39.519 --> 00:04:43.662
One more real pair of numbers,

00:04:43.662 --> 00:04:48.239
at a completely different scale.

00:04:48.239 --> 00:04:55.179
A $5M TVL pool, $1M in 30-day volume, a

00:04:55.179 --> 00:05:02.640
0.05% fee tier: 1,000,000 × 0.0005 × 365 ÷

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:09.059
5,000,000 comes out to 3.65% fee APR.

00:05:10.359 --> 00:05:12.970
One nuance worth knowing before you assume a

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.822
higher fee tier is always the better choice.

00:05:15.822 --> 00:05:18.334
A higher fee tier earns more per trade,

00:05:18.334 --> 00:05:21.475
but often attracts less volume, since traders and

00:05:21.475 --> 00:05:25.167
aggregators route toward the cheapest available price.

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:27.092
A lower fee tier earns less per

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:29.677
trade, but often wins far more volume.

00:05:29.677 --> 00:05:33.029
The fee A.P.R. formula is what actually tells you

00:05:33.029 --> 00:05:36.112
which one wins, not the fee tier number alone.

00:05:37.412 --> 00:05:38.502
Quick check.

00:05:38.502 --> 00:05:41.160
Why use a 30-day average volume instead

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.017
of today's volume, when estimating fee APR?

00:05:48.437 --> 00:05:51.717
The answer: single-day spikes overstate fee income,

00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:53.987
and they happen on exactly the days

00:05:53.987 --> 00:05:56.447
you take the most impermanent loss, so

00:05:56.447 --> 00:05:58.907
the average gives a more honest number.

00:06:00.207 --> 00:06:01.212
Worked example.

00:06:02.512 --> 00:06:05.267
Here's the pool this worked example runs on.

00:06:05.267 --> 00:06:08.106
$10M in total value locked.

00:06:08.106 --> 00:06:11.756
$2M in 30-day average daily volume.

00:06:11.756 --> 00:06:14.121
And a 0.3% fee tier.

00:06:14.121 --> 00:06:16.413
Every number that follows comes directly

00:06:16.413 --> 00:06:18.425
out of these three starting figures.

00:06:19.725 --> 00:06:22.528
Now the arithmetic, step by step, exactly

00:06:22.528 --> 00:06:24.630
as the formula lays it out.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.823
Daily pool fees: two million dollars in volume,

00:06:27.823 --> 00:06:31.282
times zero point three percent, equals six thousand

00:06:31.282 --> 00:06:34.575
dollars, earned by the entire pool, that day.

00:06:34.575 --> 00:06:37.554
Your ten-thousand-dollar deposit is zero point

00:06:37.554 --> 00:06:40.580
one percent of the ten-million-dollar pool.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.447
Zero point one percent of six thousand dollars

00:06:43.447 --> 00:06:46.070
is six dollars a day, for you specifically.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.262
Now see how sensitive that twenty-one point nine

00:06:50.262 --> 00:06:52.859
percent actually is, to the two things most

00:06:52.859 --> 00:06:55.225
likely to change after you deposit.

00:06:55.225 --> 00:06:57.822
If daily volume halves, your fee A.P.R.

00:06:57.822 --> 00:07:00.678
halves with it, straight line, since volume

00:07:00.678 --> 00:07:03.445
is a direct multiplier in the formula.

00:07:03.445 --> 00:07:05.732
And if a lot more T.V.L. joins the

00:07:05.732 --> 00:07:08.543
same pool, your share shrinks, at the same

00:07:08.543 --> 00:07:11.484
volume, so your fee income shrinks too, even

00:07:11.484 --> 00:07:14.490
though nothing about your own deposit changed.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.123
Here's exactly where to actually go find

00:07:18.123 --> 00:07:20.513
these three numbers yourself, on any pool

00:07:20.513 --> 00:07:23.545
you're considering, before you deposit a dollar.

00:07:23.545 --> 00:07:26.221
Open the pool on its own protocol's app or

00:07:26.221 --> 00:07:29.570
a pool-analytics site, not a general price chart.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.635
Read T.V.L. and the fee tier

00:07:31.635 --> 00:07:34.285
directly off the pool's own page.

00:07:34.285 --> 00:07:36.646
And pull thirty days of volume from that

00:07:36.646 --> 00:07:39.755
same analytics view, not just today's headline number.

00:07:41.055 --> 00:07:42.145
Quick check.

00:07:42.145 --> 00:07:45.318
In this worked example, what happens to your fee

00:07:45.318 --> 00:07:48.750
APR if the pool's TVL doubles, with volume unchanged?

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:55.233
The answer: your share of the pool

00:07:55.233 --> 00:07:57.414
halves, and so does your fee income,

00:07:57.414 --> 00:07:59.772
at the same volume, since more deposits

00:07:59.772 --> 00:08:02.130
are now splitting the same trading fees.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:06.127
One last honest number, before the checklist,

00:08:06.127 --> 00:08:08.648
since twenty-one point nine percent on its

00:08:08.648 --> 00:08:11.000
own can sound like the whole story.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.701
That figure is before impermanent loss and

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:16.087
before gas, both real costs this same

00:08:16.087 --> 00:08:18.460
worked example is still ignoring.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.491
Lesson two point four takes this exact pool and

00:08:21.491 --> 00:08:25.280
shows what actually survives once both are counted properly.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.040
Here's your checklist.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.245
Do it now, before any deposit.

00:08:30.245 --> 00:08:32.290
Your fee A.P.R. is calculated

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:35.045
from thirty-day volume, not today's.

00:08:35.045 --> 00:08:38.891
You're genuinely comfortable ending up holding one hundred percent

00:08:38.891 --> 00:08:42.290
of either token, since that's a real possible outcome.

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:44.596
Your entry prices are recorded, so you

00:08:44.596 --> 00:08:47.735
have an actual “versus holding” benchmark later.

00:08:47.735 --> 00:08:49.944
And your exit rule is written down

00:08:49.944 --> 00:08:52.532
now: volume drops, a clear trend starts,

00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:55.436
or fees simply stop covering impermanent loss.

00:08:56.737 --> 00:08:57.982
Let's recap.

00:08:57.982 --> 00:09:02.244
Providing liquidity means depositing both tokens and becoming the

00:09:02.244 --> 00:09:05.772
counterparty to every trade, not a passive lender.

00:09:05.772 --> 00:09:08.969
Fee A.P.R. is four numbers: daily volume, fee

00:09:08.969 --> 00:09:12.514
tier, your share, annualised, and for a full-range

00:09:12.514 --> 00:09:15.247
position it simplifies to one line.

00:09:15.247 --> 00:09:18.725
Use thirty-day volume, since single-day spikes overstate

00:09:18.725 --> 00:09:21.832
income exactly when impermanent loss is worst.

00:09:21.832 --> 00:09:24.772
And your fee A.P.R. is sensitive to both

00:09:24.772 --> 00:09:28.932
volume and T.V.L. changing after you've already deposited.

00:09:30.232 --> 00:09:33.077
Calculate the real fee APR from thirty-day

00:09:33.077 --> 00:09:35.892
volume, before any deposit, not after.

00:09:35.892 --> 00:09:39.064
Next up, Lesson 2.4: Impermanent

00:09:39.064 --> 00:09:41.082
loss and true LP P&L.
